I record and cite sources rather than advise. Vitamin K is essential for the formation of coagulation factors that work together to clot the blood when injuries to blood vessels occur (South Tees Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust). It is a substance that is naturally present in the body and plays an important part in helping blood to clot (East London NHS). It is a vitamin the body needs for blood clotting (University Hospital Southampton NHS). Good sources include green leafy vegetables such as broccoli and spinach, vegetable oils, cereal grains, and small amounts in meat and dairy (Bedfordshire, Luton and Milton Keynes NHS). Adults need approximately 1 microgram of vitamin K per kilogram of body weight each day (NHS 111 Wales).

