Vitamin K is essential for the activation of several blood coagulation (clotting) factors and thus plays a role in the normal coagulation pathway (https://www.hra.nhs.uk/planning-and-improving-research/application-summaries/research-summaries/vitamin-k-deficiency-in-patients-undergoing-hip-surgery). It is found naturally in green leafy vegetables, vegetable oils, cereal grains and, in smaller amounts, in meat and dairy foods (https://www.blmkhealthiertogether.nhs.uk/health-for-young-people/growing/healthy-diet). The recommended intake for adults is about 1 microgram per kilogram of body weight per day (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k). Taking up to 1 mg of vitamin K each day is unlikely to cause any harm (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k). Information on regulatory dosing of phytomenadione and its use in patients on warfarin is available from the BNF and NHS formulary sources (https://bnf.nice.org.uk/drugs/phytomenadione; https://www.rightdecisions.scot.nhs.uk/tam-treatments-and-medicines-nhs-highland/formularies/highland-formulary/nutrition-and-blood/vitamin-k-formulary).

