Folate is essential for DNA synthesis and the formation of genetic material in all body cells (https://www.hey.nhs.uk/wp/wp-content/uploads/2016/03/vitaminB12FolateDeficiency.pdf). It also contributes to nervous system health (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia). Good dietary sources include liver, fortified breakfast cereals, dark green leafy vegetables, legumes, citrus fruits, avocados and many others (https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/folate-information-for-renal-patients-information-to-increase-folate-in-your-diet). The recommended intake for adults is 0.2 mg per day (200 μg), with long‑term supplement use kept below 1 mg per day (https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/folate-information-for-renal-patients-information-to-increase-folate-in-your-diet; https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf).

